First-principles calculations are helpful to elucidate the interface structures of BFO related heterostructures. Huang et al. [104] investigated coherent super-tetragonal BFO/LaAlO\({}_{3}\) and rhombohedral BFO/LaAlO\({}_{3}\) heterointerfaces by transmission electron microscope and first-principles calculations. In Fig. 5a, there are three transition layers for BFO from cubic-like to super-T-like, a continuous expansion of the BFO crystal lattice, elongation of oxygen octahedra, and displacement of Fe ions at heterointerfaces. A similar pinned interface transition layer about two unit cells thick is directly observed at heterointerfaces. As shown in Fig. 5b, T-like BFO experiences compressive strain, and R-like BFO experiences tensile strain. Therefore, it exists a critical mismatch around -4.3 % relative to R-BFO. The phase stability of R-like and T-like BFO is a result of competition between the tilting of FeO\({}_{6}\) octahedra and the displacement of Fe ions, which is depended on the strain. These results are consistent with first-principles calculations.

